# Use operations from TypeScript

Operations model caller-visible asynchronous workflows.

```trellis
operation "Reports.Generate" {
  version "v1";
  input GenerateReportInput;
  progress GenerateReportUpdate;
  output GenerateReportOutput;
  cancel;
}
```

Place this declaration and its models inside the API. The service participant
implements that API; the caller selects `invoke operation "Reports.Generate"`
and `observe operation "Reports.Generate"` inside its `use required` block.
Run `trellis generate` in both projects.

Register a provider before starting the service lifecycle:

```ts
await service.handleReportsGenerate(async ({ input, op }) => {
  await op.started().orThrow();
  const output = await generateReport(input);
  return await op.complete(output).orThrow();
});
```

`generateReport` is your application function, returning `GenerateReportOutput`.
On the generated client:

```ts
const operation = await client.reportsGenerate(input).start().orThrow();
const terminal = await operation.wait().orThrow();
if (terminal.state === "completed") console.log(terminal.output);
```

Progress is durable low-frequency telemetry. An optional `update` schema carries
live-only content; it is not replayed and cannot replace authoritative terminal
output. Cancellation is cooperative and must be declared and selected by the
caller. Use jobs instead for execution that should remain service-private.
